# Redoubt LW-M3-07: preinstall the hash-pinned signed uBlock Origin XPI.
#
# Ordinary Gecko installation and the real add-on registry own extension state.
# A one-shot trusted local permission callback permits the bundled transaction;
# all other prompts remain ordinary user decisions. Durable provisioning state
# preserves user removal/disable across restarts and updates. Engine sessions
# wait for installation and a real blocking-listener readiness signal; failures
# keep browsing paused and present Retry/Close. The filter bootstrap Gecko pref
# remains independent of the APK asset path. Requires ubo-readiness.patch.
#
# Two startup paths the readiness wait must survive. (1) Gecko's ordinary add-on
# updater can replace uBO with a newer AMO-signed release while its readiness is
# awaited (first run with AMO serving a newer version); only a strictly newer,
# enabled copy with the same ID restarts the wait, bounded, with its own timeout.
# (2) On APP_STARTUP Gecko delays background pages until the first GeckoView
# window; the session middleware holds every tab, so a launcher start opens none.
# A speculative session (as IntentReceiverActivity makes for VIEW intents) opens
# that window without loading anything before the wait begins.
#
# LW-M7-44 (2026-10-06): a failed readiness wait used to fail closed whatever
# the registry showed afterwards, so the user disabling uBO in Settings > Add-ons
# while it was still loading its filters (or removing it) paused browsing behind
# "uBlock Origin setup failed" even though a restart accepts exactly that state.
# After the existing settle window, a copy Gecko marks userDisabled, or the
# removal of an already provisioned copy, now ends the wait as Ready without
# filtering. Only the user (or chrome code) sets userDisabled; WebExtensions can
# toggle only themes. Blocklist, signature and app disablement, a same-version
# reload (whose transient userDisabled has cleared by then), and removal during
# the very first installation still fail closed, as before. The registry must
# also read the same for 3 polls (750 ms) after the window, so a reload whose
# userDisabled outlasts the window on a slow device is seen restarting; one
# still changing 5 s after the window fails closed.
#
# Owner decision 2026-10-06, verbatim: "yes, put it in 158, no warning needed".
# LW-M7-44 ships with Firefox 158. A non-user disable (blocklist, signature)
# that a restart accepts as Ready(enabled=false), so browsing continues
# unfiltered, needs no warning: no notice is added for it.
#
# 157 rebase (2026-10-02, Firefox release 157.0): no behaviour change.
#   * HomeActivity.kt: 157 already imports androidx.lifecycle.Lifecycle and
#     sorts imports in plain ASCII order, so only repeatOnLifecycle,
#     AlertDialog, flow.collect and LibreWolfUboPreinstaller are added, each at
#     its sorted position. observeUboStartup() still runs right after
#     super.onCreate(); 157 inserted EdgeToEdgeFragmentLifecycleCallbacks
#     .register() there, and the call follows it, before checkAndExitPiP().
#   * strings.xml: the four strings are appended at the new end of the file.
#   * WebExtensionSupport(.Test), FenixApplication, Components, Core: offsets
#     only. The AC/GeckoView APIs this uses (Middleware typealias,
#     CreateEngineSessionAction, listInstalledWebExtensions,
#     speculativeCreateSession, AddonManager.installAddon, Metadata) were
#     compared 153.4 -> 157 and are unchanged apart from formatting.
#
# 158 rebase (2026-10-04, pre-rebase against Firefox 158.0b3): only the
# strings.xml hunk moved. It appends the four librewolf_ubo_setup_* strings at
# the end of Fenix's strings.xml, and 158 appended its own reader-mode strings
# there, so the trailing context changed. Same strings, same place (last
# before </resources>). Everything else is unchanged.
